for n,q,steps,ans,p in [
 ('28','Find the separate lines represented by 5x²+3xy−8y² = 0.',['Identify a = 5, h = 3/2 and b = −8.','Compute D = h²−ab = 9/4+40 = 169/4.','Thus √D = 13/2.','The plus factor is 5x+(3/2+13/2)y = 5x+8y.','The minus factor is 5x+(3/2−13/2)y = 5x−5y, equivalent to x−y.','Check directly: (x−y)(5x+8y) = 5x²+3xy−8y².','A product is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero, so these two lines give the complete real zero set.'],'5x+8y = 0 and x−y = 0.',208),
 ('29','Find the angle between the lines x²−xy−6y² = 0.',['Factor the quadratic: (x−3y)(x+2y) = 0.','The lines have slopes m₁ = 1/3 and m₂ = −1/2.','Their slope difference magnitude is 5/6 and 1+m₁m₂ = 5/6.','Hence tan α = 1 for the smaller unsigned angle α, giving α = 45°.','The other intersection angle is 180°−45° = 135°.','Independently, a = 1, h = −1/2, b = −6 gives D = 25/4 and atan2(5,5) = 45°.','The source’s signed quotient is −1; its 135° answer uses a supplementary branch. The principal arctangent alone returns −45°.'],'Smaller angle 45°; supplementary angle 135°.',209),
 ('30','Find the two first degree straight lines represented by 3x²−4xy−3y² = 0 and determine whether the resultant lines are coincident or perpendicular.',['Identify a = 3, h = −2, b = −3.','Compute D = (−2)²−3(−3) = 13 > 0, so the two real lines are distinct.','The plus line is 3x+(−2+√13)y = 0.','The minus line is 3x+(−2−√13)y = 0.','The product of their linear forms is 9x²−12xy−9y², equal to 3 times the source quadratic.','The product of their y coefficients is (−2+√13)(−2−√13) = −9.','Their slopes therefore have product 9/(−9) = −1, proving perpendicularity. Equivalently a+b = 0 gives a right angle.'],'3x+(−2+√13)y = 0 and 3x+(−2−√13)y = 0; angle 90°.',209)]:
 EXAMPLES.append({'num':n,'title':'Homogeneous Line Pair','question':q,'steps':steps,'answer':ans,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':p,'jpg':'108_page_2.jpg' if p==208 else '109_page_1.jpg','verification':'Complete source example and final linear factors visually checked'}]})
